  "claims": [
    {
      "card_ids": ["C20"],
      "claim": "Nemonoxacin is completely absorbed after oral administration with approximately 100% oral bioavailability in humans, demonstrating that a small-molecule oral drug with an ionizable amine can achieve essentially complete absorption.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C23"],
      "claim": "Entecavir has 100% absolute oral bioavailability in humans, supporting high F for a small, moderately polar oral drug.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C10"],
      "claim": "Minocycline is reported to have 100% oral bioavailability with rapid absorption, another small-molecule amine-containing drug supporting high oral F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "Posaconazole has 96% oral bioavailability in humans, supporting high F for a lipophilic oral drug.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C29"],
      "claim": "Gabapentin has about 60% absolute oral bioavailability, still well above the 20% threshold, supporting high classification for a small amino-containing drug.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C24"],
      "claim": "Amphotericin B has negligible oral bioavailability due to poor permeability, low solubility, and gastric instability, but this large polyene macrolide is structurally dissimilar to the small adamantane amine and does not transfer to the query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is 1-aminoadamantane (amantadine), a small lipophilic primary amine. Multiple high-transferability records from small-molecule oral drugs with ionizable amines or lipophilic cores show essentially complete or high oral bioavailability (nemonoxacin 100%, entecavir 100%, minocycline 90-100%, posaconazole 96%, gabapentin 60%). The only clearly poor-F evidence is amphotericin B, whose large polyene macrolide structure and permeability/solubility liabilities do not transfer to the query. Therefore F >= 20% is strongly supported.",
